   229	- **The ferry's rig (flag).** The scow hangs from the cable by two running blocks, one over her bow and one over her stern, each on **a strap of plaited hide as long as a man** to a post at her rail. In slack water she crosses "set a little aslant on her two straps, so that the current itself shouldered her along the cable"; the hauling rope from her bow turns through a block lashed to **an oak post at the landing's head** and so into the shed. The drum has **an iron dog** (a pawl) hinged to the shed's king-post. The ferryman had two hauling ropes "before the snow"; the second, sent across by a skiff when the first strands, is the sixty yards owed. The old slip's **sill** lies under the mud two strides south of the post. In flood the cable bellies to the south; she crossed light in three minutes and came home in twenty-two. The rope has "two yarns gone where she bore on the sheave". The new stern strap is rove out of the tail of the stern line. The fare is a copper a head.
