   239	- **Vastin as he arrives (flag):** a drover's coat of brown frieze, too short in the sleeve, bought "at the last market inside the Line"; the grey one left on its hook; an ash stick cut out of a hedge; a pack with one strap mended in cord; a small purse on a cord; the flat carried under the pack against his back, and **a single copied page** inside his coat in oilcloth. **The knee** "had begun to complain at about the hundredth mile and had left off being civil at the two hundredth"; no side is given; ten miles a day. He left "the seal of his office in the tray marked *done*, some months ago, on a morning of rain, with nothing written to go beside it". At the last station he looks in at the plate and remembers only what Book 5 shows: a plate, a file and a set of questions carried up a bluff, "and all three had told him nothing"; and that he declined a second reading and never said why. He crossed the sill on the 265th and reached the western landing on the evening of the 276th. His left hand would not open after the strap and has not lain flat since. He has paid a copper for the fare, a copper and a half for a loaf, a copper for a bowl and a copper for the loft. He sleeps in **the loft over the tap, up the keeper's stair**.
