 succeeded in 0ms:
   130	
   131	He had been on the north scree, above the sink, the whole time — Cael understood it afterward, drawing it — a body on a slope not using anything, ten strides above the plane's north end, and he had come down the scree onto the road in the breath after the heap, and was in it, with the knife, in the runnel, on the ice, where the pack was, doing the thing he did without a sound. Brom came in from the south end with the rock. Lira came down the scree a second time. Cael came up the road from the west with the knife into the back of the heap, and it was not a fight then; it was the runnel, and steel, and four people who did not declare, and a wall.
   132	
   133	It took a moment. When it was done there was ice with grey on it, and three hounds going back over the road's north edge into the sink at a run because a pack that has lost five is not a pack, and Brom on the stone with the rock, and Lira on the scree with one hand on the slope and the other in the air where she had put it to keep her balance and had not needed to, looking at the hand.
   134	
   135	And the wall came down.
   136	
   137	Not down. *Off.* The plane stopped being stone and was air, and the man who had held it went down on the stone behind Brom like a man who has been holding a thing up and has been told he can stop, both knees, and then a hand, and then he was sitting on the road with his back against the cut's south wall and his eyes open and his face the colour of the ice, and Oryn was already coming down the cut from the east end with the mule's rope dropped behind her, because she had seen where the casualties would be and had been right.
   138	
   139	---
   140	
   141	The costs, while she worked, because the costs were the ledger and the ledger was his.
   142	
   143	Brom: a hound's tooth through the boot, the right one, through the leather and into the foot above the small toes, not deep — the hound had been dying when it bit — and Brom sitting on the stone with the boot off and the foot in Oryn's hands saying, "It's a boot," in the voice he used for a thing that had cost him money, and Oryn saying, "It's a foot," and binding it. The shoulder: carried through all of it, still and forward, and not once used, and Cael saw Oryn look at it while her hands were on the foot and say nothing, again, which was twice.
   144	
   145	Lira: nothing. Two arms. She had used both. She was standing at the cut's south wall opening and closing the left hand and looking at it, and she caught him looking, and stopped.
   146	
   147	Seln: nothing, and the knife wiped, and gone back up the north scree to look into the sink at the three that had gone into it, and come back, and said, "Three. They'll den somewhere else. Tell the factor the sink's clear, and tell him it's clear because it's empty, not because it's safe."
   148	
   149	Himself: the burst's bill, which he knew the price of to the copper — breath, gone; the ten frameworks knocking together in him like a cart on a bad road, which would last the day; the ribs, which had been quiet since the marsh and were quiet still, which he noted, because a thing that stayed quiet was a thing you wrote down. The still place at the middle of them, untouched, unmoved, going around nothing. He did not read it. He had not been asked to.
   150	
   151	And Vastin.
   152	
   153	Oryn came to him last, because he was the one who was not bleeding, and knelt on the road in front of him with her hands on her knees and looked at him — the count, and the finding — and said, "How long did you hold it."
   154	
   155	"Until it was done."
   156	
   157	"That's not a length."
   158	
   159	"I don't know the length." He said it plainly, in the ledger voice, the one thing he was still certain of. "I have never held it longer than a field needed. A field needs it for the length of a charge. This was not a field."
   160	
   161	"No." Oryn put two fingers on the inside of his wrist, which was the one thing she did to everyone and the only thing she had ever done to him, and held them there, and took them away. "You're spent to the floor. And the knee's gone — not broken; gone the way a knee goes that's been asked to be a wall's foot on stone for as long as that was. That's the wound, and it's a real one, and it'll be a real one for a month. You held a thing across a road with nothing behind it but a man with a rock, for as long as eight animals wanted to come through it, and you'd have held it until you fell, and you nearly did." She stood up. "Don't do that again. Not here. In a field, whatever you like. Here, a wall with nothing behind it is a man alone in a road being loud."
   162	
   163	Vastin looked past her, up the cut, at the ice with the grey on it, and at Brom on the stone with his boot off, and at Lira on the wall with her hand, and at Seln coming back down the north scree, and at Cael, standing on the road where the boy had stopped being loud.
   164	
   165	"The wall held the line," he said. Not to Oryn. To the road. "And someone else had to be the reason they wanted the line." He was quiet a moment. "I have never once in forty years needed someone else to be the reason."
   166	
   167	"You do now," Cael said.
   168	
   169	"Yes." Vastin looked at his own hands, open on his knees. "I know fields. I don't know packs." He picked the stick up off the road, and did not stand, because his legs had not agreed to it yet. "I'll learn or I won't."
   170	
   171	Nobody answered that. It was not a thing that wanted an answer. Oryn went back to Brom's foot, and Karis came down the cut with the mule and the records under her arm and looked at the ice and the grey and the plane that was not there any more, and said, to the notebook, "Note the day," and wrote it, and the cut was a cut again, with a road through it that somebody kept.
   172	
   173	---
   174	
   175	*Two hundred and seventy-ninth day. The cut below the shoulder hold, on the border road. Clearance: done; the hold's twenty. Five. Three into the sink; the factor told the truth about it. Witness: the factor, at his gate, who paid what the slip said and wrote it in a book of his own and did not ask for a name. A hundred and thirty-eight in hand. Nothing owed.*
   176	
   177	*Injuries, all seven, because I do this: Brom, right foot, a tooth through the boot, bound; the shoulder carried, not used, not read. Lira, nothing; two arms; used both. Karis, nothing. Seln, nothing. Oryn, nothing; she was where she said she'd be. Me, the burst — breath, the frameworks for a day, a locked landing beat, spent on a slope on purpose, with nothing in it but me being still, the ribs quiet. Vastin, spent to the floor, and the knee — the wall's foot, on stone, for as long as it took — gone under him; Oryn bound it and said a month. That's the wound.*
   178	
   179	*Vastin fought. He rooted the road and nearly got eaten for it — a wall's no good if you're the only thing making noise behind it. He knows fields. He doesn't know packs. He'll learn or he won't. Watched an Iron Wall go up two feet from me. Filed it. Note the day: an Archmarshal took a wound out here and there was no one to report it to. He looked almost free. Almost.*

