    87	"They all know what a road is. They come up from the river quiet as a burying, two days, and never a finger lit; I've had Iron men walk through that gate who'd let a beast die in the shafts on the moor sooner than bring up the grey. They know. Then they come into my cut." A hand went out flat, and tilted. "There's a seep at the head of it. It comes down the middle of the road, a stride wide, and from the first frost till the last it's glass from top to bottom, in a place the sun never finds. A wheel goes onto that. Then the cart begins to go, backward, down a hill, with a year's goods on her and a man's own two beasts in front. What does the man do?"
    88	
    89	"He holds it," said Brom slowly.
    90	
    91	"He's Iron, or he's Stone, or he's some small thing that he was given at fourteen and has kept in his pocket for two days. He's had it out before he's thought. Carters declare, on the ice." The factor spat over the wall. "And then they come up out of the sink."
    92	
    93	"How many?" said Seln.
    94	
    95	"There's a hole below the road on the north side, where that water goes into the hill. They lie up round it. I've seen five. I'd not swear to five, and nobody who's been nearer has come back to tell me better."
    96	
    97	Cael heard that, and he saw that Seln had heard it too.
    98	
    99	"They never came up for a cart that kept its feet," the factor went on. "Not once. A man could lead a mule through there at noon with her head held, and I've known it done all the cold term by them that had the nerve. It's the wheels. I can't salt a byre off a mule's back."
   100	
   101	"And the terms?" said Cael.
   102	
