   109	From the hold's back wall a sheep-walk ran out along the spur's crest, and at its end the ground stopped.
   110	
   111	Cael lay on his stomach there with Lira on his left and Seln on his right, and looked down thirty feet of scree into the cut. He had learned in a library, from a list of other people's bouts, that a place is fought in before it is stood in, and he had never had a better seat to learn one from.
   112	
   113	It was a hundred strides long, as they had been told, and it rose from his left hand to his right, from east to west, by perhaps the height of two men in its whole length. The road in the bottom of it was no wider than a cart and a man walking beside. On the near side, the south, the bank was this same scree that he lay at the head of, loose plates of the dark stone lying at the pitch of a steep roof. The north bank was low. A man could have stepped up onto it; and beyond it the ground fell away again, steeply, down more of the same broken stone, to a hollow.
   114	
   115	"Fifty-five yards," said Seln, under his breath. "From the road's edge to the water. I have counted it three times against that thorn."
   116	
   117	The hollow was round, like a bowl, and it lay in the spur's shadow, where the day never came. There was ice on it. The ice was grey and dull, with water standing on it at the edges, and Cael knew that grey: the shelf at Lowmarch had gone that colour a week before the river took it. At one side, under a lip of rock, there was a black place where it had not shut, and he could hear water going down there, a long way, into the hill.
   118	
   119	Round that bowl's rim the stone had been scooped. There were hollows in it, each the length of a hound, worn as smooth as the seat of an old chair.
