    60	
    61	That was the second cost, and it was arriving already. It was going out of him as heat goes out of a forge when the door is opened, steadily, with nothing kept back. Cael's own Arbiter had been dark since the day that it woke, and yet he felt it from two feet away. He had stood beside Brom when the grey came up, and beside Karis with nine points alight, and neither had been a tenth of this. Every hound in that cut could feel it from the far side of the hill. It was the loudest thing that had been done on that road in the memory of the den.
    62	
    63	It did not slide away down the ice, either, and it did not stop. It stood.
    64	
