   195	"One thing more, before these are shut," said Karis. "I have a figure for every row, and I made each of them twice; but I made them both, and a count that one person has made is only a hope. I want a second hand on each. Take a row. Count its sheets, by the corners, and say nothing until you have the figure. Then I shall tell you mine."
   196	
   197	She gave the first row to Lira, who read everything with her lips and would count so. The third went to Brom, who dried his hands on his breeches a second time before he would touch it. Seln had gone to the basin, and stood there looking at the water with his back to the slab; she set the fourth by for him, and took the sixth herself, since it was the oldest and the softest.
   198	
   199	The second she slid across the rock to Cael.
   200	
   201	He looked at it. It lay in its loosened tape, a heap of printed sheets as thick as two fingers, and it was the first thing that anybody had put into his hands to be measured since a cord in a yard. He had been waiting all morning to be passed over. He saw now that she had not passed him over, and that she had done it on purpose: a small pale woman had thought about whether to give him a row to count, and had given it.
   202	
   203	"By the corners," she said, without looking up. "They are thin."
   204	
   205	He counted them. It was not hard. He lifted each sheet by its lower corner with one finger, and let it fall upon the last, and said the number in his head; the stock was soft, and it clung a little, and twice he went back three sheets to be sure that he had not lifted two together. He came to the row's foot, and to a figure.
   206	
   207	He did not trust it. He turned the whole heap over, face down, and counted it again from the back.
   208	
   209	"Lira," said Karis.
   210	
   211	Lira gave her figure.
   212	
   213	"That is mine. Brom."
   214	
   215	Brom gave his, and it was hers.
   216	
   217	"Cael."
   218	
   219	He told her what he had. He saw her pencil go to the place in the notebook, and stop there.
   220	
   221	"Again," she said, quite gently.
   222	
   223	"I've done it twice. From the front and from the back." His mouth had gone dry. "It's the same."
   224	
   225	"Then you are one short of me."
   226	
   227	The wind came round the end of the rock and lifted the corner of the uppermost sheet, and let it down. Nobody at the slab moved. Lira had turned her head; Brom had his thick fingers flat on the third row, as if it might be the next to go.
   228	
   229	Cael looked at his own two hands, which lay on either side of a heap of paper. It was a cord that he was thinking of, run out three times across an inn yard, with a slip of ash laid down at its end and a scratch on a stone trough under the middle of the slip. It was a hair, in a gap that had been measured for it. He had been one short then. He had held the thing exactly as four other people held it, and it had come out a piece under what it was.
   230	
   231	He had thought that paper would be different, since a sheet either lay in a heap or did not.
   232	
   233	"Count it for me," he said. "With your own hands. I'll not touch it."
   234	
   235	Karis had begun to rise from her stone. She had the look that he had seen on her in a store behind a chimney, with her cheek almost on the deal, and it was not unkind; it was a great deal worse than that. It was careful.
   236	
   237	A sleeve came past his shoulder.
   238	
   239	He had not heard anybody cross the turf. A small dry hand laid a single sheet down on the rock in front of him, between his two hands, and squared it with one finger, and went away again. It was thin stock, and newer than the rest, and it had been folded once. It had a printed head. At its foot a bar of black lay across the paper, as broad as a thumb, cracked like mud in a rut.
   240	
   241	It had not been laid at the foot of the second row, where it belonged, and it had not been given to Karis, who was the keeper of it. It had been set before him. And the man who had set it there did not say one word.

