editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:5:The wind could not reach them there. It went over the top of the outcrop with a steady hollow noise, like breath across the mouth of a jar, and now and then a little of it came round the rock's end and lifted a sheet's edge by the width of a finger. The sun stood a hand above the eastern moor. It was a thin white light, with no warmth in it, and it showed every ruled line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9:She slipped each tape in turn, and went through the row beneath it by the corners, with her gloved finger and her lips; and she did it twice. She wrote each figure in her notebook under the row's place, and said none of them aloud. It took her the better part of an hour. Nobody hurried her. Lira brought pebbles from the basin's overflow in the lap of her coat, a great many more than were wanted, and set them along the slab's edge in a row, by size.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:25:She had gone back to the uppermost row. She went through it a third time, quickly, looking only at the heads, and she laid a finger of her left glove on the rock for every group of four signs that she had not yet met in that row. The thumb went down last. She did the same with the second row, and the third, and so to the sixth, and each time the thumb went down, and no sixth finger was wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:53:Karis put one gloved finger at the head of the nearest sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:89:"The place. He has written it against the fifth column, under the word that they all write." Her finger lay beside the ruled space, not on it. "He was a man who lived among instruments. He knew how a made thing behaves: it does the same at every hour, for every person who comes to it, and it has an edge that you could rule. That is what he found at the line. And then he has written that it cannot be one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:173:"No. And it is nothing to do with size." She laid her finger on the sheet whose measure was double. "This place is twice the others, and it has the same stroke and the same bar as they have, drawn no larger and no differently. Whatever that square is for, it is not for how large a thing is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:201:He looked at it. It lay in its loosened tape, a heap of printed sheets as thick as two fingers, and it was the first thing that anybody had put into his hands to be measured since a cord in a yard. He had been waiting all morning to be passed over. He saw now that she had not passed him over, and that she had done it on purpose: a small pale woman had thought about whether to give him a row to count, and had given it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:205:He counted them. It was not hard. He lifted each sheet by its lower corner with one finger, and let it fall upon the last, and said the number in his head; the stock was soft, and it clung a little, and twice he went back three sheets to be sure that he had not lifted two together. He came to the row's foot, and to a figure.
