books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md:15:The rest of the halt went on around the slab as a halt goes on. Oryn came down off the bank once, to look at Brom's foot without being asked, and put two fingers on the binding and said, "It'll walk," and went back up, and did not look at the flat, because paper was not a thing she read and she had said so at a table. Brom looked at the flat the way he looked at a wall somebody else was pricing — with respect, and no intention of touching it. Lira went past on the road above, and down again below, and on the third pass stopped at the trough and drank from her two hands, and looked at the left one after, and went on. The mule ate. The spring ran. Seven people and an animal at a carter's halt on a kept road, with six years of a continent's hidden weather on a slab between them and a rise to the west with a city behind it, and nobody, Cael noted, looking west.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md:61:"Here." Her finger above a sheet in the first row — the Ring — not on it. "Top corner, by the stamp. A mark. The stroke and the bar." She moved along the row. "Same mark. Same mark. Every Ring sheet, every year." The second row. "The Stair — the stroke and the bar. The Hall — the stroke and the bar." Her finger stopped over the fourth row. "The fourth. The stroke and the bar. The same. Twice the size and the same mark." She went on down the rows, the ones that were theirs, the sites the crew had never found, and her finger went over them and she did not say what was on them, because she had not checked, and a number said once was a number said. "It's the kit's mark. The designation in the field ledger — the entry I couldn't read, in the index I don't know — it's this. The same stroke and the same bar. The office puts it in the corner of every survey like a tier, and the kit's man put it in his ledger like a tier, and it *is* one; it's a grade; they've ranked these — by something — and the fourth's ranked the same as the three though it's twice their size, and I can't read it." She took her hand back. "I can see it's a rank. I can't see what it ranks them by, and I can't see why a thing twice the size gets the same mark, and I'm not going to guess. That's all I can say, and I'm not going to say more, because the next thing I'd say would be a guess wearing a finding's coat."
