89:"The place. He has written it against the fifth column, under the word that they all write." Her finger lay beside the ruled space, not on it. "He was a man who lived among instruments. He knew how a made thing behaves: it does the same at every hour, for every person who comes to it, and it has an edge that you could rule. That is what he found at the line. And then he has written that it cannot be one."
139:It was the first thing since he had learned to hold a pencil that he had known and had not entered. He was a little afraid of how easy it was.
33:"There's a thing that none of you has asked me in two hundred and eighty days, and I've been glad of it. I'll say it once, since it bears on what I want of him." She did not look at Cael. "I wasn't made out here. I was made in a city, inside the Line. It doesn't matter which; they're all one city, with different rivers. There was a house there for the sick, with forty beds and six of us to tend them, and I'd my rank from it."
61:"Here." Her finger above a sheet in the first row — the Ring — not on it. "Top corner, by the stamp. A mark. The stroke and the bar." She moved along the row. "Same mark. Same mark. Every Ring sheet, every year." The second row. "The Stair — the stroke and the bar. The Hall — the stroke and the bar." Her finger stopped over the fourth row. "The fourth. The stroke and the bar. The same. Twice the size and the same mark." She went on down the rows, the ones that were theirs, the sites the crew had never found, and her finger went over them and she did not say what was on them, because she had not checked, and a number said once was a number said. "It's the kit's mark. The designation in the field ledger — the entry I couldn't read, in the index I don't know — it's this. The same stroke and the same bar. The office puts it in the corner of every survey like a tier, and the kit's man put it in his ledger like a tier, and it *is* one; it's a grade; they've ranked these — by something — and the fourth's ranked the same as the three though it's twice their size, and I can't read it." She took her hand back. "I can see it's a rank. I can't see what it ranks them by, and I can't see why a thing twice the size gets the same mark, and I'm not going to guess. That's all I can say, and I'm not going to say more, because the next thing I'd say would be a guess wearing a finding's coat."
103:Cael did not look at Seln. There was a thing between the two of them now that neither had said and neither was going to, and it lay in the second row of a dead office's file with a bar of ink under it, and it was, he thought, the first thing he had ever owned that he had not written down, and he found that he could carry it.
25:"And west is a city." She turned the cup in her hands without drinking from it. "I've been in a city. I was certified in one. I'll not say which; it doesn't matter which; they're all the one city when you're a healer in them. I healed for a year inside walls, and the walls had a ledger, and the ledger had a column for the patient's tier, and I was assigned by the column. Bronze and above first. I was told that on the first day as if it were a kindness — *'You'll not be wasted on the yard, girl'* — and I spent a year mending Bronzes with bruises while the yard went septic under the window, and I left. I walked out of a gate with a roll on my back and I have not been inside a gate that shuts since, and I'm not going inside one now to stand in a square with a pass in my hand that says what rank I'm let to bleed on." She looked up from the fire, then, at Cael, and at nobody else. "That's not a complaint. I don't make those. It's a finding about a healer, so you'll know what kind you've got, and it's this: I'm not going to be talked into a city, and I'd take it as a courtesy if nobody tried."

